---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';

/*
  Parked 2026-09-14 (research/site-audit-2026-09-14.md, "/story/"). Reduced the same day to a
  short introduction built only from what his DD-214 documents and his own transcribed words
  that answer no open interview question. No unit, no dates beyond the form's, no names of the
  men he served with, nothing from the founding brief. Sources for each sentence:
  - Drafted, inducted 28 Oct 1963; Signal school, 15 weeks, 1964, radio teletype operator;
    PFC 29 Jun 1964; SP4 15 May 1965; Armed Forces Expeditionary Medal (Vietnam); last duty as a
    patient under the Army medical command in Japan; released Oakland 30 Nov 1965, honorable:
    data/sources/dd214-1965.json, research/records-request/dd214-findings.md.
  - Fort Ord, Fort Gordon near Augusta: giscus-vn-0024-c18119890 (transcribed).
  - Nha Trang quotations: giscus-vn-0025-c18032317, giscus-vn-0018-c18415793 (transcribed).
  - The Mountain: 2026-09-01-phone-the-mountain-name; "I liked it there. It was safer there.":
    giscus-vn-0044-c18259616 (transcribed).
  - Camp Zama: giscus-stories-c18006833 (transcribed).
  The timeline link needs /timeline/ restored first (or removed) — check-published fails on
  links into parked sections.
*/
---

<BaseLayout title="Larry's Story">
  <span class="eyebrow">An introduction</span>
  <h1>Larry's Story</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Larry Dennis Hunnewell was drafted into the U.S. Army in October 1963 and spent about a year
    of his service in Vietnam. This page is a short introduction. The story itself is in his
    own words, with the pictures he brought home.
  </p>

  <p>
    He was inducted at Los Angeles on October 28, 1963. He did basic training at Fort Ord,
    California, and went on to radio school at Fort Gordon, Georgia, outside Augusta: a
    15-week Signal course that made him a radio teletype operator. He was promoted to Private
    First Class in June 1964.
  </p>

  <p>
    He spent his first two months or so in Vietnam at Nha Trang, on the coast. In his words,
    &ldquo;they didn't know where to put us&rdquo;. He calls it &ldquo;the most
    beautiful place you ever saw&rdquo;. Later he served stints on a mountain he calls The
    Mountain: &ldquo;I liked it there. It was safer there.&rdquo; He was promoted to
    Specialist 4 in May 1965.
  </p>

  <p>
    He left Vietnam sick, and was a patient in Japan, at the Army hospital at Camp Zama, for the
    rest of his service. He was honorably released from active duty at Oakland, California, on
    November 30, 1965. His record lists the Armed Forces Expeditionary Medal for Vietnam.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <a href={withBase('/timeline/')}>timeline</a> puts what he remembers in order. Sixty
    years on, he is going back through that year with the
    <a href={withBase('/photos/')}>photographs</a> he brought home, the
    <a href={withBase('/footage/')}>film</a>, and the <a href={withBase('/drawings/')}>drawings</a>
    he has made from memory. If something comes back to you, you can
    <a href={withBase('/memories/')}>tell a story</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }
</style>
